  <div class="section-label">Corridor Preservation</div>
  <h2>Advance Acquisition Program</h2>

  <p>UDOT's Corridor Preservation Program has been acquiring properties in western Weber County in anticipation of eventual construction. Under Utah Code 72-5-402, this is explicitly a <strong>voluntary process</strong> — UDOT cannot use eminent domain for corridor preservation on an unfunded project.{% cite "utah-72-5-402" %} The draft study says UDOT "has consistently supported the 5100 West alignment through corridor preservation," and the chosen hybrid alignment uses 33 parcels UDOT already owns.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-appendix-nov2026" %} UDOT's draft 2027–2032 program lists about $40.3 million in state corridor-preservation funds for the "West Davis Corridor; Corridor Preservation — North Legacy Corridor" line, including about $35.5 million in 2027,{% cite "udot-draft-stip-2027-2032" %} and the Utah Transportation Commission's September 25, 2026 agenda included corridor-preservation items at 4000 South in West Haven and on SR-177 in Hooper.{% cite "utc-agenda-sep2026" %}</p>

  <p>In some cases, land has been secured through developer dedication agreements. Weber County accepted a <strong>300-foot wide freeway parcel</strong> dedication from the Westbridge Meadows developer as part of its development agreement — 50 feet wider than UDOT's stated 250-foot standard footprint.{% cite "weber-westbridge-dedications" %}</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>How to track the State Environmental Study:</strong> Register for study updates at <a href="https://udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension" rel="noopener noreferrer">udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension</a>. Property owners can cross-reference their parcel against the proposed right-of-way in the Draft State Environmental Study's Volume 2 mapbook.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol2-nov2026" %} Public comment is accepted through December 11, 2026, including at the November 10 public hearing at Roy Junior High School.{% cite "udot-ses-public-hearing-nov2026" %}</p>
  </div>
